    "agent_messages": [
      "I added `POST /purchases/bulk-reject` and registered it in `protocol.Routes`. It builds, vets and passes `go test ./...`, but none of it has run against a database. There are no tests in the repo and no database credentials were available, so the transaction and concurrency behaviour is reasoned from the code, not observed. I only changed `internal/candidate/approval.go`.\n\n**How it's built:** I moved the existing bulk-approve code into one shared function, and both endpoints now use it. They differ only in the operation name (`bulk-approve.v3` / `bulk-reject.v4`), the response field (`approvedIds` / `rejectedIds`) and the new status (`approved` / `rejected`). The bulk-approve SQL, locking and responses are unchanged. Body, identity, size limit and Idempotency-Key checks stay in the shared `protocol.HTTP` adapter.\n\n**What reject does, all in one transaction:**\n1. It sets the RLS session values (tenant, user, role) from the authenticated identity, so client context headers can't override it.\n2. It takes a lock on operation + tenant + actor + key. If a matching `ActionRequest` already exists, it returns the stored response with `Idempotency-Replayed: true`; a different target set returns 409. Different actors or tenants using the same key don't affect each other.\n3. It locks the target rows with `FOR UPDATE`. Every target must be in the current tenant, created by someone else and still `submitted`; otherwise the whole batch gets 409.\n4. It sets the status to `rejected` and writes one `ApprovalAudit` row per target. This matches the existing \"every status change is audited\" rule, and the v4 schema allows it.\n5. It writes exactly one `ActionRequest` row with the response `{\"rejectedIds\":[...]}`. If that insert fails, the whole transaction rolls back and the client gets 503, so the same key can be retried later.\n\n**Concurrency, as designed:**\n- **Same key, same targets:** the key lock makes the second request wait, find the stored result and replay it, so both get 200 and only one write happens.\n- **Overlapping batches, or approve vs reject on the same target:** the row locks plus the re-checked `status='submitted'` condition make the loser get 409, with nothing written. The same check stops a rejected order from being approved or rejected again, and an approved one from being rejected.\n\n**One deliberate fix:** the old fingerprint helper always used `bulk-approve.v3` as its prefix. I replaced it with `ledger.Fingerprint(op, ids)`. That gives the same hash for approve, and a hash for reject that the nightly reconciliation check will accept; the old helper would have made it flag every reject row as tampered."
    ],
